WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.135
If there really is one piece of music in this world

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:05.105
that everyone must hear at least once in their lifetime,

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:09.042
then for me, it would be Mahler’s Symphony No. 2, “Resurrection.”

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:09.642
Why?

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:11.544
Because it’s unbelievable.

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.813
This piece is absolutely overwhelming.

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:29.129
Mahler once said,

00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:32.732
“A symphony must be like an entire world.”

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:35.068
“It must embrace everything.”

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:36.536
So in his symphonies,

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.338
he always pushed everything to the extreme.

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:41.307
Huge orchestras, massive choirs—

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:44.511
sometimes with hundreds or even thousands of performers onstage.

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.080
But none of that is Mahler’s greatest achievement.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.315
What truly makes Mahler extraordinary

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.851
is that he wasn’t merely writing music.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:55.021
He was exploring profound philosophies of life.

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:57.090
In this “Resurrection” Symphony,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.894
he confronts the questions none of us can escape:

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:03.129
What is death?

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.798
Where do we go after we die?

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:07.434
And what is the meaning of being alive?

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.702
I don’t know if you ever feel this way too,

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:13.473
but living in this world can feel incredibly tragic.

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.075
You’re always working overtime for your boss,

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.242
always paying off a mortgage,

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.510
crushed by the weight of life,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:18.978
unable to see any hope for the future.

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.481
And just when you finally accumulate some wealth and success,

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:24.184
fate strikes without warning

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:26.753
and takes everything away from you.

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:27.887
And after we die,

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.023
there’s nothing we can take with us.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.825
Everything feels empty.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:33.593
Everything feels meaningless.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.895
So what was all our effort for?

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.298
Did we come into this world just to suffer?

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:40.133
In the “Resurrection” Symphony,

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.701
Mahler, just like you and me,

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:44.437
struggles with these despairing questions.

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:47.006
Through this symphony that lasts over an hour,

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:49.409
he lets us witness his painful struggle

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:51.444
and the answer he ultimately found.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:52.712
Many people mistakenly think

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.948
this is simply a religious work.

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:58.151
But in reality, it’s far more complex than that.

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:01.221
The ideas Mahler expresses go far beyond religion,

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:03.590
blending together many philosophical currents of his time—

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.925
Goethe, Schopenhauer, Nietzsche.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:08.595
Through them, he shows us how these great thinkers,

00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:11.564
after a lifetime of pain and struggle,

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:14.000
arrived at their understanding of life’s meaning.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.469
If you truly understand what this work is about,

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.972
then even if you have no religious faith at all,

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.841
this music will still move you to tears

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.110
and rekindle your passion for life.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.045
So today, through this video,

00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:30.083
I want to show you why you absolutely must hear Mahler’s “Resurrection.”

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:46.332
This is huge, everyone.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.767
On November 15 this year,

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.737
Simon Rattle will lead the Bavarian Radio Symphony Orchestra

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:53.806
in Mahler’s “Resurrection” at Weiwuying in Kaohsiung.

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:56.309
And tickets sold out immediately after going on sale.

