In case you didn’t know,

00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:03.249
the Bavarian Radio Symphony Orchestra

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.685
is consistently ranked among the world’s finest orchestras.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.020
And Simon Rattle needs no introduction—

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.922
one of the world’s most renowned conductors.

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:10.356
For anyone who loves music,

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:13.459
I honestly can’t think of a single reason to miss this concert.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.794
And it’s Mahler 2.

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:15.428
My god.

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.297
For Mahler fans, this is a no-brainer.

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:20.733
Honestly, even if you’ve never attended a live symphony concert before,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.237
I think this would be an incredible introduction.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:29.008
Because it lets you directly experience the irresistible power
of Mahler’s symphonies.

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.711
But first, you should finish this video.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:35.415
Mahler’s music is admittedly quite profound.

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:36.683
But don’t worry too much,

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.619
because I’ll guide you through the entire context—

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:41.988
both musically and philosophically.

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.122
After watching this video,

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:45.091
you’ll be able to walk into the concert hall yourself

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:48.428
and experience that overwhelming, spine-tingling sensation.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.530
Anyway, all the concert information is below.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:51.731
Get ready to grab tickets.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.767
And if you use our channel’s promo code, you’ll get a special discount too.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:58.137
Now then, let’s dive deep into this symphony.

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:00.440
The most moving moment in Mahler’s Second Symphony—

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.674
those who know, know—

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.376
is the passage you just heard.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.245
The very, very, very, very, very last part

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:06.846
right before the end of the work:

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:08.548
the Resurrection Chorus.

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:11.451
But if you truly want to understand why that chorus is so moving,

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:13.653
you have to hear everything that comes before it—

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:16.422
to witness the struggles of each movement

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:19.359
before you can understand what “resurrection” truly means.

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:21.094
So let’s begin with the first movement.

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:23.963
Honestly, the first movement alone is already a masterpiece worthy of endless discussion.

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:27.233
Right from the start, it confronts the greatest question of life:

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:28.167
death.

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:30.603
Because this movement is a funeral march.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.672
Mahler even once gave it the title

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.641
“Totenfeier” — Funeral Rites.

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:37.277
In most people’s minds,

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:40.713
a funeral should be solemn, sorrowful, dignified.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:41.381
Right?

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:45.151
Everyone dressed in black, quietly mourning the deceased.

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:47.820
But Mahler’s funeral is completely different.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.722
Rather than solemnity and grief,

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:53.092
it feels filled with seething anger.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:17.183
Why is this funeral so emotionally charged?

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:19.218
In the violent notes of the cellos,

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:22.488
it feels as though Mahler is asking a piercing question:

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:25.925
Why were we brought into this world to suffer?

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:28.528
And who exactly is he accusing?

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:30.530
Take a moment to think about that.

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.366
In any case, through astonishing orchestration,

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:35.401
Mahler gives voice to these accusations.

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:37.570
You hear each section gradually enter:

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.372
first the cellos and basses,

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:42.342
then the high woodwinds, brass, violins—

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:46.145
until the entire orchestra finally erupts into a furious cry.

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:13.399
Wow. How extraordinary.

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:16.536
Mahler’s use of every instrument is absolutely seamless.

00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:20.106
Each part interlocks perfectly, building tension until it finally explodes.

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:22.475
And yet, just as the anger reaches its peak,

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:26.045
the atmosphere suddenly shifts into an entirely different melody.

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:43.696
This melody is so beautiful it almost takes your breath away.

00:07:43.696 --> 00:07:47.300
It feels like those precious moments of beauty in life

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:48.901
that we never want to let go of.

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:49.769
But listen carefully.

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:51.938
You’ll realize it isn’t purely beautiful.

00:07:51.938 --> 00:07:57.543
Its sweetness is tinged with bitterness, and it could collapse again at any moment.

00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:18.798
From this point on, the movement constantly swings between these two opposing emotions—

00:08:18.798 --> 00:08:22.201
a longing for life and the agony of emptiness,

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:25.071
locked in endless conflict with each other.

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:28.407
And because the music keeps shifting between emotional extremes,

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:32.178
the funeral march, which should feel steady and weighty,

00:08:32.178 --> 00:08:35.515
instead undergoes violent and unusual tempo changes.

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:38.651
One moment it’s solemn and slow, the next it suddenly surges forward.

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:39.418
It’s unbelievable.

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:41.287
Wouldn’t the funeral procession itself

00:08:41.287 --> 00:08:42.622
become total chaos?

00:08:42.622 --> 00:08:45.958
So rather than depicting the footsteps of the procession,

00:08:45.958 --> 00:08:49.395
Mahler is clearly portraying the turmoil inside the participants themselves.

00:08:49.395 --> 00:08:52.098
Mahler himself once wrote:

00:08:52.098 --> 00:08:55.134
“We stand beside the grave of a deeply beloved person.”

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:59.272
“His life, his struggles, his suffering, and his achievements”

00:08:59.272 --> 00:09:01.407
“pass before our eyes once more.”

00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:05.578
“And at this moment, a voice we usually ignore”

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:09.448
“amid the illusions of daily life asks us:”

00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:10.616
“What next?”

00:09:10.616 --> 00:09:13.252
“What is life? What is death?”

00:09:13.252 --> 00:09:14.887
“Do we continue to exist forever?”

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:17.089
“Is all of this merely a chaotic dream,”

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:21.093
“or do life and death actually hold meaning?”

00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:25.731
“If we are to keep living, we must answer these questions.”

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:28.200
But how could such questions ever be easy to answer?

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.605
The deeper we search, the more life seems to be nothing but suffering and emptiness,

00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:35.675
while death appears to be the only release.

00:09:35.675 --> 00:09:38.544
So why should we keep living at all?

00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:41.981
Throughout this movement, you hear Mahler’s exploration of these questions

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:45.651
as a long journey filled with torment and struggle.

00:09:45.651 --> 00:09:49.956
After hearing the conflict between the two extreme themes in the exposition,

00:09:49.956 --> 00:09:51.657
the development section

00:09:51.657 --> 00:09:54.360
raises these questions to another level entirely.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:55.294
Listen carefully.

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:59.632
The low strings play an unbearably heavy tread.

00:10:06.505 --> 00:10:10.810
They move forward slowly, like walking corpses.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:14.914
At the same time, you can hear the English horn playing a figure that sounds like weeping.

00:10:23.022 --> 00:10:25.558
This sounds just like a person's lamentation.

00:10:25.558 --> 00:10:28.394
Sigh...

00:10:37.303 --> 00:10:40.840
So who exactly is walking and lamenting at the same time?

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:42.775
Is it the funeral procession from earlier?

00:10:42.775 --> 00:10:44.210
Actually, no.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:45.978
Things are no longer that simple.

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:48.414
If you keep listening, you will gain more clues.

00:10:48.414 --> 00:10:52.485
Because here, Mahler directly quotes a very ancient chant melody,

00:10:52.485 --> 00:10:55.888
which is none other than the famous "Dies Irae" (Day of Wrath).

00:11:07.233 --> 00:11:09.602
The first four notes of this melody you hear

00:11:09.602 --> 00:11:12.471
are taken directly from the "Dies Irae" theme.

00:11:12.471 --> 00:11:15.007
The original chant sounds like this:

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.653
And its text is the one used in Catholic funerals,

00:11:27.653 --> 00:11:28.754
the sequence text.

00:11:28.754 --> 00:11:32.758
What it describes is a highly important event in the Christian worldview:

00:11:32.758 --> 00:11:34.026
The Last Judgment.

00:11:34.026 --> 00:11:35.394
The content is roughly like this:

00:11:35.394 --> 00:11:37.697
On that day of wrath,

00:11:37.697 --> 00:11:40.066
the world will be burned into ashes.

00:11:40.066 --> 00:11:43.169
And all the dead will rise from the ashes,

00:11:43.169 --> 00:11:44.870
summoned by the sound of trumpets,

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:46.572
and line up before God

00:11:46.572 --> 00:11:48.808
to receive the most severe judgment,

00:11:48.808 --> 00:12:01.220
deciding who goes to heaven and who goes to hell.
And everything you did during your lifetime will be fully exposed,
with no crime able to escape punishment.

00:12:05.758 --> 00:12:07.760
Therefore, this text is used in funerals

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.562
to admonish believers through fear:

00:12:09.562 --> 00:12:13.332
while alive, one must constantly repent, atone, and glorify God,

00:12:13.332 --> 00:12:15.401
otherwise you will not have a good end after death.

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:17.603
Consequently, its melody in Western civilization

00:12:17.603 --> 00:12:19.138
is frequently used to symbolize

00:12:19.138 --> 00:12:21.207
the fear of death.

00:12:21.207 --> 00:12:22.575
Knowing these things,

00:12:22.575 --> 00:12:26.145
when we come back to listen to this marching music accompanied by the Dies Irae,

00:12:26.145 --> 00:12:27.346
you will understand

00:12:27.346 --> 00:12:30.416
that these are not the footsteps of mourners at all,

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:33.519
but a marching procession heading toward the Last Judgment.

00:12:33.519 --> 00:12:37.790
It is as if the people attending the funeral also begin to remember the world after death,

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:41.293
imagining that they too, one day, will drag their heavy steps

00:12:41.293 --> 00:12:43.963
before God to face judgment.

00:13:01.413 --> 00:13:02.948
But don't you think it makes no sense?

00:13:02.948 --> 00:13:05.518
Haven't we suffered enough in the human world?

00:13:05.518 --> 00:13:07.653
Why must we undergo this kind of punishment?

00:13:07.653 --> 00:13:13.259
And should the meaning of our lifetime of struggle
be defined by this judgment?
That is too pathetic.

00:13:13.259 --> 00:13:17.129
Thus, sorrow and fear eventually morph into anger and defiance,

00:13:17.129 --> 00:13:19.064
causing this procession to completely break down

00:13:19.064 --> 00:13:23.369
and letting us hear the most agonizing grievance and roar in history.

00:14:21.994 --> 00:14:23.629
Wow.

00:14:23.629 --> 00:14:27.266
Is it not incredibly, incredibly, incredibly stunning?

00:14:27.266 --> 00:14:32.071
Truly, only the greatest genius in history could write music like this.

00:14:32.071 --> 00:14:33.639
So, returning to the question at the beginning:

00:14:33.639 --> 00:14:37.409
who do you think these accusatory roars are directed at?

00:14:37.409 --> 00:14:39.612
In fact, this is closely related to the origin

00:14:39.612 --> 00:14:42.414
of the movement's original title, Totenfeier.

00:14:42.414 --> 00:14:43.883
This word actually comes from

00:14:43.883 --> 00:14:48.587
the German translation of the dramatic poem Dziady (Forefathers' Eve) by Polish writer Mickiewicz.

00:14:48.587 --> 00:14:51.523
This work is hailed as the greatest romantic literary piece

00:14:51.523 --> 00:14:54.026
after Goethe and Byron.

00:14:54.026 --> 00:14:56.729
The story inside reflects the 19th-century Polish people

00:14:56.729 --> 00:14:59.565
suffering under the brutal rule of the Russian Tsar,

00:14:59.565 --> 00:15:00.966
living in unspeakable misery.

00:15:00.966 --> 00:15:02.701
Some might comfort themselves by saying

00:15:02.701 --> 00:15:05.170
that these hardships are trials arranged by God.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:07.873
But others hated God for this.

00:15:07.873 --> 00:15:10.075
Thus, when the protagonist of the story is arrested

00:15:10.075 --> 00:15:11.510
and thrown into prison,

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:14.280
he accuses God to his face, saying:

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:18.817
"You are not the Father of the world—You are the Tsar!"

00:15:18.817 --> 00:15:21.253
Can you imagine? In an era where faith was paramount,

00:15:21.253 --> 00:15:23.822
someone actually dared to speak such blasphemy against God.

00:15:23.822 --> 00:15:27.293
It goes to show that the resentment in his heart must have reached an unmanageable state.

00:15:27.293 --> 00:15:31.397
Coincidentally, Mahler himself once quoted this phrase directly in a letter:

00:15:31.397 --> 00:15:33.098
"When I am conducting these works,

00:15:33.098 --> 00:15:36.735
I am always deeply shaken by a strange awe, condensing a burning thought:

00:15:36.735 --> 00:15:38.871
What kind of world is this?

00:15:38.871 --> 00:15:42.541
The storm following the funeral march is like an accusation against the Creator.

00:15:42.541 --> 00:15:44.410
And in my works of this period,

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:47.479
such a call is raised time and again:

00:15:47.479 --> 00:15:51.350
You are not the Father, You are the Tsar!"

00:15:54.186 --> 00:15:55.087
In this first movement,

00:15:55.087 --> 00:15:58.290
the orchestration Mahler employs is simply incredible,

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.894
and his depiction of various intense and complex emotional conflicts is unbelievable.

00:16:01.894 --> 00:16:05.764
It is hard to imagine that this music was written by a young man who was only twenty-eight.

00:16:05.764 --> 00:16:07.166
However, in this short video,

00:16:07.166 --> 00:16:09.501
I cannot analyze all the details for you.

00:16:09.501 --> 00:16:13.405
To get the most complete experience, you will have to go to the concert hall and feel it yourself.

00:16:13.405 --> 00:16:16.108
Originally, Mahler only intended to publish this first movement

00:16:16.108 --> 00:16:18.277
independently as a symphonic poem,

00:16:18.277 --> 00:16:20.379
with the title Totenfeier mentioned earlier.

00:16:20.379 --> 00:16:21.547
But as you can see,

00:16:24.416 --> 00:16:27.019
there are still too many questions left unanswered in this movement,

00:16:27.019 --> 00:16:28.020
and too much resentment left unsoothed.
So Mahler ultimately decided

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.857
that he must write the subsequent movements to resolve these massive problems.

00:16:31.857 --> 00:16:32.925
Because of this,

00:16:32.925 --> 00:16:35.728
what was originally a single-movement symphonic poem

00:16:35.728 --> 00:16:39.898
became a fully realized, multi-movement symphony.

00:16:39.898 --> 00:16:42.067
After the indignant first movement concluded,

00:16:42.067 --> 00:16:43.669
Mahler personally instructed

00:16:43.669 --> 00:16:46.005
that a cooling-off period of five minutes must pass

00:16:46.005 --> 00:16:48.674
before continuing to play the second movement.

00:16:48.674 --> 00:16:51.810
Evidently, this was because the emotions of the first movement were so intense

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:53.946
that some time was needed to settle down

00:16:53.946 --> 00:16:55.981
before we could continue on the rest of the journey.

00:16:55.981 --> 00:16:57.816
But as you know, five minutes...

00:16:57.816 --> 00:16:58.550
are you kidding me?

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:00.285
With such a long duration, how can you ensure the audience

00:17:00.285 --> 00:17:02.654
won't get so bored that they pull out their phones to scroll through TikTok or Threads?

00:17:02.654 --> 00:17:03.889
And what about the musicians on stage?

00:17:03.889 --> 00:17:05.290
What are they supposed to do? Pick their noses?

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:07.526
So nowadays, very few people actually follow this instruction.

00:17:07.526 --> 00:17:09.962
However, as a high-quality music analysis channel,

00:17:09.962 --> 00:17:11.830
we must, of course, respect the composer's intentions.

00:17:11.830 --> 00:17:14.600
So, let's start counting five minutes from right now...

00:17:15.534 --> 00:17:16.402
Alright, just kidding.

00:17:16.402 --> 00:17:19.705
After five minutes of cooling down, we arrive at the second movement.

00:17:19.705 --> 00:17:22.474
Its mood is completely different from before.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:45.097
This is a very simple and conservative folk dance.

00:17:45.097 --> 00:17:46.565
To be more precise,

00:17:46.565 --> 00:17:49.134
it is a Ländler.

00:17:49.134 --> 00:17:52.237
This is a traditional dance popular among Austrian peasants,

00:17:52.237 --> 00:17:55.107
and it is also the music Mahler himself grew up listening to.

00:17:55.107 --> 00:17:58.377
Through this music, you can almost see a rustic village

00:17:58.377 --> 00:18:00.879
where the villagers, uncles, aunts, and elders

00:18:00.879 --> 00:18:03.515
are singing, dancing, laughing, and playing in the meadows, taverns, and markets.

00:18:05.884 --> 00:18:08.987
Everything feels so simple, so joyful.

00:18:08.987 --> 00:18:13.192
You can hardly imagine that just moments ago we were hearing him curse God.

00:18:13.192 --> 00:18:14.927
But at the same time, you can also notice

00:18:14.927 --> 00:18:18.697
that the music remains at an extremely soft volume throughout.

00:18:18.697 --> 00:18:21.033
As if everything were very far away,

00:18:21.033 --> 00:18:23.502
like an unreal and illusory dream.

00:18:23.502 --> 00:18:25.304
Just as Mahler himself explained,

00:18:25.304 --> 00:18:29.675
this movement is like reminiscing about the small moments of the deceased's life.

00:18:29.675 --> 00:18:33.145
At this moment, we temporarily forget all the anger

00:18:33.145 --> 00:18:35.948
and all the unanswerable questions.

00:18:40.953 --> 00:18:43.422
However, this beautiful dream does not last very long.

00:18:43.422 --> 00:18:44.623
By the third movement,

00:18:44.623 --> 00:18:47.192
we are immediately jolted awake by a harsh drumbeat.

00:18:47.192 --> 00:18:49.261
We have to face reality once again.

00:19:11.717 --> 00:19:14.186
If we look at it from the structure of a traditional symphony,

00:19:14.186 --> 00:19:17.756
the third movement is usually arranged as a scherzo.

00:19:17.756 --> 00:19:21.193
Its character is often humorous, mischievous, cute, and lighthearted,

00:19:21.193 --> 00:19:25.564
meant to balance out the heavier atmosphere of the other movements.

00:19:25.564 --> 00:19:28.367
But when you listen to this movement, although it can still be called “humorous,”

00:19:28.367 --> 00:19:31.370
it is not the cute, cheerful, hahaha kind.

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:33.772
It is something much darker and more ironic...

00:19:33.772 --> 00:19:35.674
more of a sinister “hehehe” kind of humor.

00:19:35.674 --> 00:19:37.442
So what exactly is Mahler mocking?

00:19:37.442 --> 00:19:38.610
If you dig a little deeper,

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:40.979
you will discover that this movement actually has a fascinating background.

00:19:40.979 --> 00:19:43.649
Its main melody is directly borrowed from

00:19:43.649 --> 00:19:48.353
one of the songs in Mahler’s own song collection, 《Des Knaben Wunderhorn》.

00:19:48.353 --> 00:19:50.789
“St. Anthony’s Sermon to the Fishes.”

00:19:55.627 --> 00:19:59.198
The texts in 《Des Knaben Wunderhorn》 were mostly collected from folk legends.

00:19:59.198 --> 00:20:01.099
And this song is roughly about

00:20:01.099 --> 00:20:02.668
a saint named Anthony.

00:20:02.668 --> 00:20:04.536
He was supposed to preach at a church,

00:20:04.536 --> 00:20:06.104
but when he arrived,

00:20:06.104 --> 00:20:08.073
he found the church completely empty.

00:20:08.073 --> 00:20:10.809
“Huh? What happened? Where did all my followers go?”

00:20:10.809 --> 00:20:12.477
“Well, since I’m already here,

00:20:12.477 --> 00:20:14.479
why not find some new listeners to preach to?”

00:20:14.479 --> 00:20:18.050
So he walked to the riverside and preached to the fish in the river.

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:21.320
At first, the fish gathered around him with their mouths wide open,

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:23.055
as if they were listening with great interest.

00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:24.623
But after the sermon ended,

00:20:24.623 --> 00:20:26.558
they all swam away empty-headed,

00:20:26.558 --> 00:20:28.660
forgetting everything they had heard,

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:30.829
continuing on exactly as before.

00:20:30.829 --> 00:20:32.631
Once you understand the meaning of these lyrics,

00:20:32.631 --> 00:20:35.834
you realize how full of irony this song really is.

00:20:35.834 --> 00:20:39.905
The piano accompaniment imitates the way fish swim through the river.

00:20:49.381 --> 00:20:52.551
Later, Mahler reworked this song for orchestra,

00:20:52.551 --> 00:20:53.752
removing the vocals,

00:20:53.752 --> 00:20:55.387
and greatly expanding the material,

00:20:55.387 --> 00:20:58.490
turning it into the third movement of his Symphony No. 2.

00:21:07.099 --> 00:21:09.901
Listen to this rapid figure that originally depicted schools of fish.

00:21:09.901 --> 00:21:14.306
Here it sounds like lifeless machinery endlessly grinding away,

00:21:14.306 --> 00:21:17.809
like some blind and aimless will,

00:21:17.809 --> 00:21:19.778
pursuing something without knowing what.

00:21:25.584 --> 00:21:31.123
To me, this resembles the pessimistic philosophy proposed by the famous philosopher Schopenhauer.

00:21:31.123 --> 00:21:33.292
Schopenhauer believed that the essence of the world

00:21:33.292 --> 00:21:36.862
was not something deliberately created by some god,

00:21:36.862 --> 00:21:39.631
but rather driven by a blind will.

00:21:39.631 --> 00:21:42.934
It has no purpose, no end, and can never be satisfied.

00:21:42.934 --> 00:21:44.903
So for us humans living in this world,

00:21:44.903 --> 00:21:48.240
we are constantly chasing after things blindly.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:50.809
When we fail to attain them, we suffer.

00:21:50.809 --> 00:21:54.746
But even when we finally attain them, we quickly become bored and dissatisfied again.

00:21:54.746 --> 00:21:57.749
In the end, life becomes like a pendulum,

00:21:57.749 --> 00:22:01.420
swinging endlessly between boredom and suffering.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:02.821
So listen carefully to this music:

00:22:02.821 --> 00:22:05.357
those endlessly flowing notes,

00:22:05.357 --> 00:22:09.728
don’t they resemble the blind, purposeless,

00:22:09.728 --> 00:22:12.764
ever-striving will that Schopenhauer described?

00:22:26.812 --> 00:22:28.780
Perhaps we are all like the fish in the river,

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:30.382
living busy and mediocre lives.

00:22:30.382 --> 00:22:32.551
Or perhaps we are like that preacher,

00:22:32.551 --> 00:22:34.786
never really knowing what we are pursuing,

00:22:34.786 --> 00:22:36.655
always wasting our efforts.

00:22:36.655 --> 00:22:39.024
What a despairing reality that is.

00:22:39.024 --> 00:22:40.592
And so, in the latter half of this movement,

00:22:40.592 --> 00:22:43.528
Mahler wrote a violent, collapsing scream

00:22:43.528 --> 00:22:45.664
that did not exist in the original song.

00:23:03.382 --> 00:23:07.652
This kind of breakdown reflects the fin-de-siècle crisis

00:23:07.652 --> 00:23:09.554
facing Europeans at the end of the 19th century.

00:23:09.554 --> 00:23:11.556
Back in the era dominated by Christianity,

00:23:11.556 --> 00:23:14.760
people could still find meaning in life through faith.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.095
They believed God had arranged everything in the world,

00:23:17.095 --> 00:23:19.765
and that human beings existed to glorify God,

00:23:19.765 --> 00:23:21.833
and to return to Him after death.

00:23:21.833 --> 00:23:25.737
But with the Industrial Revolution, the Enlightenment, and other social transformations,

00:23:25.737 --> 00:23:28.440
people began to worship reason and science,

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:30.675
gradually abandoning religious faith.

00:23:30.675 --> 00:23:32.644
Without God as the purpose of life,

00:23:32.644 --> 00:23:36.214
people suddenly no longer knew what they were living for.

00:23:36.214 --> 00:23:38.316
Material life improved,

00:23:38.316 --> 00:23:39.985
but life itself became mechanized.

00:23:39.985 --> 00:23:41.787
The inequality brought by capitalism,

00:23:41.787 --> 00:23:44.489
along with class exploitation, became suffocating.

00:23:44.489 --> 00:23:46.258
As a result, nihilism began to spread,

00:23:46.258 --> 00:23:48.727
and Schopenhauer’s pessimistic philosophy seized the opportunity.

00:23:48.727 --> 00:23:51.897
He not only told us that suffering is the essence of life,

00:23:51.897 --> 00:23:54.266
but also argued that the only way to escape suffering

00:23:54.266 --> 00:23:56.635
was to deny the will to live itself.

00:23:56.635 --> 00:23:58.870
This deeply shocked many people at the time,

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:00.405
causing them to question the meaning of suffering,

00:24:00.405 --> 00:24:02.140
and the value of life itself.

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:05.076
All of Europe fell into a severe existential crisis.

00:24:05.076 --> 00:24:07.913
But at the same time, some people refused to give up.

00:24:07.913 --> 00:24:09.948
For example, the philosopher Nietzsche.

00:24:09.948 --> 00:24:12.050
He tried to rediscover the value of life

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:14.286
within this vast emptiness.

00:24:14.286 --> 00:24:17.322
And Mahler also devoted tremendous effort to this search.

00:24:17.322 --> 00:24:20.725
So after enduring the suffering and collapse of the earlier movements,

00:24:20.725 --> 00:24:22.427
we finally arrive at the fourth movement,

00:24:22.427 --> 00:24:24.763
where we glimpse a faint ray of hope.

00:24:24.763 --> 00:24:28.934
For the first time in his symphonic works,

00:24:28.934 --> 00:24:31.002
Mahler introduces the human voice.

00:24:31.002 --> 00:24:33.672
Through words sung by a human voice,

00:24:33.672 --> 00:24:37.075
he has something he wants to tell us directly.

00:24:37.075 --> 00:24:40.111
The vocal line begins with three ascending notes,

00:24:40.111 --> 00:24:42.681
as if reaching upward in search of something.

00:24:54.426 --> 00:24:56.761
And after those three notes, the melody continues extending upward,

00:24:56.761 --> 00:24:58.997
as though it were flying all the way to heaven.

00:25:10.041 --> 00:25:12.143
This song is likewise taken directly

00:25:12.143 --> 00:25:15.247
from another song in Mahler’s 《Des Knaben Wunderhorn》.

00:25:15.247 --> 00:25:17.449
Its title is “Urlicht.”

00:25:17.449 --> 00:25:19.751
This song is extraordinarily beautiful.

00:25:19.751 --> 00:25:21.353
I would love to analyze it in detail for you,

00:25:21.353 --> 00:25:22.921
but due to time,

00:25:22.921 --> 00:25:25.891
I will just briefly point out the most important meaning in the lyrics.

00:25:25.891 --> 00:25:28.093
The lyrics are roughly divided into two sections.

00:25:28.093 --> 00:25:30.862
The first section echoes the suffering we experienced earlier.

00:25:30.862 --> 00:25:34.633
It says: “Humanity is in the greatest distress,

00:25:34.633 --> 00:25:37.669
humanity is in the greatest suffering.

00:25:37.669 --> 00:25:42.140
How deeply I wish to be in heaven.”

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:43.441
These words reveal to us

00:25:43.441 --> 00:25:45.510
that Mahler, caught in the fin-de-siècle crisis,

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:47.479
amid the pain of extreme nihilism,

00:25:47.479 --> 00:25:49.414
ultimately still had to seek comfort

00:25:49.414 --> 00:25:52.851
through heaven and through God,

00:25:52.851 --> 00:25:55.487
through a world beyond this one.

00:25:55.487 --> 00:25:58.690
Rather than saying that we “believe” in God’s existence,

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.559
it feels more like we “need” God to exist.

00:26:00.559 --> 00:26:03.228
And this active sense of need

00:26:03.228 --> 00:26:03.929
becomes even more apparent in the second section.

00:26:03.929 --> 00:26:06.298
It says:

00:26:06.298 --> 00:26:08.500
“And I came upon a broad road,

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:09.668
and there came a little angel,
who wanted to send me away.”

00:26:09.668 --> 00:26:13.972
And next comes one of the most crucial lines in the entire piece.

00:26:13.972 --> 00:26:16.942
Oh no! I will not let myself be driven away!

00:26:16.942 --> 00:26:18.610
I came from God,

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:21.680
and I shall return to God once again.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.281
You can see that this expresses

00:26:23.281 --> 00:26:25.083
a spirit of active determination.

00:26:25.083 --> 00:26:26.451
It reminds many people of

00:26:26.451 --> 00:26:29.821
the story in Chapter 32 of Genesis

00:26:29.821 --> 00:26:31.523
from the Old Testament.

00:26:31.523 --> 00:26:32.424
One night,

00:26:32.424 --> 00:26:35.193
a mysterious man suddenly came and wrestled with Jacob.

00:26:35.193 --> 00:26:35.927
Yes, you heard that right.

00:26:35.927 --> 00:26:37.696
Literal wrestling.

00:26:37.696 --> 00:26:38.930
The full-on grappling kind.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:40.532
They struggled together all night long,

00:26:40.532 --> 00:26:41.566
neither able to win.

00:26:41.566 --> 00:26:42.734
Until dawn finally came,

00:26:42.734 --> 00:26:44.069
and the man could not take it anymore.

00:26:44.069 --> 00:26:46.638
He said, “Daybreak is here. Let me go.”

00:26:46.638 --> 00:26:49.174
But Jacob clung tightly to him and said,

00:26:49.174 --> 00:26:51.977
“I will not let you go unless you bless me.”

00:26:53.078 --> 00:26:53.645
As it turned out,

00:26:53.645 --> 00:26:55.313
the man was actually an angel.

00:26:55.313 --> 00:26:58.316
The story sounds bizarre and almost absurd,

00:26:58.316 --> 00:27:00.151
yet it conveys something deeply important.

00:27:00.151 --> 00:27:03.355
The things we seek will not simply fall from heaven.

00:27:03.355 --> 00:27:07.225
We must actively fight for them with our own hands.

00:27:07.225 --> 00:27:10.095
That spirit is absolutely central to this symphonic poem.

00:27:10.095 --> 00:27:13.898
And it stands in sharp contrast to the obedience emphasized for centuries by the European Church.

00:27:13.898 --> 00:27:16.234
Even if an angel tries to drive me away,

00:27:16.234 --> 00:27:17.702
I will not obediently submit.

00:27:17.702 --> 00:27:21.172
Because our lives today are filled with emptiness and suffering.

00:27:21.172 --> 00:27:25.477
More than ever, we must actively search for new hope,

00:27:25.477 --> 00:27:29.681
search for that primal light capable of bringing eternal happiness.

00:27:29.681 --> 00:27:33.118
But is that primal light really the answer we seek?

00:27:33.118 --> 00:27:36.521
And how exactly are we supposed to find it?

00:27:36.521 --> 00:27:39.658
The final movement will reveal the answer.

00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:41.993
When Mahler was composing his Second Symphony,

00:27:41.993 --> 00:27:43.728
the first four movements came together rather quickly.

00:27:43.728 --> 00:27:44.963
And as you’ve just seen,

00:27:44.963 --> 00:27:47.399
he raised enormous questions within them:

00:27:47.399 --> 00:27:48.867
What is death?

00:27:48.867 --> 00:27:50.368
What is the meaning of life?

00:27:50.368 --> 00:27:51.469
After posing these questions,

00:27:51.469 --> 00:27:53.304
he had always intended for the final movement

00:27:53.304 --> 00:27:55.206
to answer them in a grand and overwhelming way.

00:27:55.206 --> 00:27:56.574
That is why the structure of the final movement

00:27:56.574 --> 00:27:59.611
resembles the last movement of Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony.

00:27:59.611 --> 00:28:02.881
It begins with an expansive instrumental exposition,

00:28:02.881 --> 00:28:04.749
restating those difficult questions.

00:28:04.749 --> 00:28:06.751
Then, in the latter recapitulation section,

00:28:06.751 --> 00:28:07.986
the choir finally enters,

00:28:07.986 --> 00:28:10.155
giving the answer through the human voice.

00:28:10.155 --> 00:28:11.790
But although the structure was planned,

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:15.093
he still could not find a convincing philosophical answer.

00:28:15.093 --> 00:28:17.495
So the fifth movement remained painfully unfinished.

00:28:17.495 --> 00:28:19.330
Until one day, through a strange twist of fate,

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:21.900
he was struck as if by lightning,

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:23.635
and suddenly found the answer to these questions.

00:28:23.635 --> 00:28:26.438
Only then was he finally able to complete the final movement.

00:28:26.438 --> 00:28:27.806
So what was that answer?

00:28:27.806 --> 00:28:29.641
Let us listen to the final movement

00:28:29.641 --> 00:28:31.543
and see how he tells us.

00:28:31.543 --> 00:28:32.811
At the very beginning of the final movement,

00:28:32.811 --> 00:28:33.578
we immediately hear it:

00:28:33.578 --> 00:28:36.448
the apocalypse has truly arrived.

00:28:52.163 --> 00:28:54.933
This opening sound, like heaven and earth collapsing,

00:28:54.933 --> 00:28:57.635
is exactly the same anguished cry

00:28:57.635 --> 00:28:59.571
we heard earlier in the third movement.

00:28:59.571 --> 00:29:02.540
They are literally the same chord.

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:05.143
But back then, it was only a cry against life’s emptiness.

00:29:05.143 --> 00:29:08.913
This time, however, the end of the world has truly come.

00:29:08.913 --> 00:29:12.484
After this, an extremely important leitmotif appears.

00:29:12.484 --> 00:29:16.087
It is a melody that keeps rising upward,

00:29:16.087 --> 00:29:19.624
so we call it the “Ascension Motif.”

00:29:30.568 --> 00:29:32.237
This Ascension Motif

00:29:32.237 --> 00:29:34.672
seems to echo what we discussed in the previous movement:

00:29:34.672 --> 00:29:38.676
the longing to fly to heaven in search of the primal light.

00:29:38.676 --> 00:29:41.412
But in traditional Christian doctrine,

00:29:41.412 --> 00:29:44.315
entering heaven is not such an easy thing.

00:29:44.315 --> 00:29:47.118
It requires passing through a brutal process,

00:29:47.118 --> 00:29:49.654
which is what we discussed in the first movement:

00:29:49.654 --> 00:29:51.356
the Last Judgment.

00:29:51.356 --> 00:29:53.158
On the day of the apocalypse,

00:29:53.158 --> 00:29:56.060
the entire world will be burned to ashes.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:59.430
All the dead shall rise from those ashes,

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:01.065
summoned by the sound of trumpets,

00:30:01.065 --> 00:30:03.134
and line up before God

00:30:03.134 --> 00:30:05.170
to face the harshest judgment.

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:07.939
Everything you did in life will be exposed.

00:30:07.939 --> 00:30:11.009
No sin will escape punishment.

00:30:11.009 --> 00:30:11.810
In other words,

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:13.211
at the moment of the Last Judgment,

00:30:13.211 --> 00:30:14.379
God will decide for you

00:30:14.379 --> 00:30:16.648
whether your life truly had meaning,

00:30:16.648 --> 00:30:18.249
and whether you are worthy of heaven.

00:30:18.249 --> 00:30:20.785
And if not, then down to hell you go.

00:30:20.785 --> 00:30:23.188
It is an absolutely terrifying idea.

00:30:23.188 --> 00:30:26.991
So here, Mahler uses the entire orchestra as his paintbrush,

00:30:26.991 --> 00:30:30.595
depicting the process of the Last Judgment in vivid detail.

00:30:30.595 --> 00:30:32.664
He employs many different leitmotifs

00:30:32.664 --> 00:30:37.101
to symbolize various scenes and concepts within the judgment itself.

00:30:37.101 --> 00:30:39.504
First, you hear the very familiar melody

00:30:39.504 --> 00:30:43.007
representing judgment itself: the “Dies Irae.”

00:31:04.262 --> 00:31:05.530
Then the “Dies Irae”

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:08.399
flows seamlessly into another extremely important melody,

00:31:08.399 --> 00:31:11.169
the Resurrection Motif.

00:31:24.582 --> 00:31:25.950
Let me give you a little preview first.

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:27.585
You can probably tell from the name itself

00:31:27.585 --> 00:31:32.323
that the Resurrection Motif is the most important motif in the entire symphonic poem.

00:31:32.323 --> 00:31:34.926
Because it directly represents the central theme of the work,

00:31:34.926 --> 00:31:37.795
the very answer Mahler ultimately discovers.

00:31:37.795 --> 00:31:39.530
There’s another thing you should notice as well.

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:43.401
You will hear this Resurrection Motif several times throughout the movement,

00:31:43.401 --> 00:31:44.602
but every time it appears,

00:31:44.602 --> 00:31:48.907
it remains tightly bound to the “Dies Irae,” the motif of judgment.

00:31:48.907 --> 00:31:50.174
What does that imply?

00:31:50.174 --> 00:31:51.342
Think about it for a moment.

00:31:51.342 --> 00:31:52.043
To me,

00:31:52.043 --> 00:31:55.546
it seems to suggest a traditional Christian idea:

00:31:55.546 --> 00:31:58.583
there can be no resurrection without judgment.

00:31:58.583 --> 00:32:00.985
This idea will develop in a crucial way later on,

00:32:00.985 --> 00:32:03.554
so keep it in mind.

00:32:03.554 --> 00:32:04.555
Not long afterward,

00:32:04.555 --> 00:32:06.658
another motif appears.

00:32:06.658 --> 00:32:08.793
It is filled with descending semitones.

00:32:20.038 --> 00:32:20.672
Remember?

00:32:20.672 --> 00:32:22.807
We already heard it in the first movement.

00:32:22.807 --> 00:32:23.675
Back then, we said

00:32:23.675 --> 00:32:27.845
that these descending semitones sound like the sighs of someone crying.

00:32:27.845 --> 00:32:29.180
Sign...

00:32:30.448 --> 00:32:32.617
Sign...

00:32:35.486 --> 00:32:36.688
And so we call it

00:32:36.688 --> 00:32:38.256
the Crying Motif.

00:32:38.256 --> 00:32:41.225
These semitones intertwine and pile up between different voices,

00:32:41.225 --> 00:32:43.528
as if someone standing before judgment

00:32:43.528 --> 00:32:46.764
is crying harder and harder until it becomes sobbing.

00:33:07.285 --> 00:33:10.555
So, these are the most important motifs in this movement.

00:33:10.555 --> 00:33:15.193
Throughout this exposition section, they are introduced one by one by the orchestra.

00:33:15.193 --> 00:33:16.995
And after all this preparation in the exposition,

00:33:16.995 --> 00:33:19.897
the real development is about to begin.

00:33:19.897 --> 00:33:24.936
The terrifying Last Judgment is about to commence.

00:33:36.881 --> 00:33:39.183
First we hear trumpet calls representing fear,

00:33:39.183 --> 00:33:40.852
mixed with weeping semitones,

00:33:40.852 --> 00:33:42.286
coming from every direction.

00:33:42.286 --> 00:33:44.288
Then the four notes of the “Dies Irae,”

00:33:44.288 --> 00:33:46.391
together with all the motifs you’ve just heard,

00:33:46.391 --> 00:33:47.825
pile on top of one another,

00:33:47.825 --> 00:33:50.361
unfolding into a march both magnificent and terrifying.

00:34:28.566 --> 00:34:29.267
Do you remember?

00:34:29.267 --> 00:34:30.601
Back in the first movement,

00:34:30.601 --> 00:34:33.071
we also heard a brief but terrifying march.

00:34:33.071 --> 00:34:34.872
At the time, it was merely the mourners at the funeral

00:34:34.872 --> 00:34:37.642
imagining the scene of the Last Judgment in their minds.

00:34:37.642 --> 00:34:38.443
But here,

00:34:38.443 --> 00:34:40.445
everything seems to become reality.

00:34:40.445 --> 00:34:42.980
This march has evolved into its complete form.

00:34:42.980 --> 00:34:44.982
It is immense and overwhelming,

00:34:44.982 --> 00:34:46.584
the entire procession of judgment vividly unfolding before us,

00:34:46.584 --> 00:34:48.419
as though it were truly happening right before our eyes.

00:34:48.419 --> 00:34:50.354
As Mahler himself described it:

00:34:50.354 --> 00:34:52.290
“A great summons rings out.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:53.825
The graves burst open.

00:34:53.825 --> 00:34:58.529
All living beings crawl out from beneath the earth, wailing and trembling.

00:34:58.529 --> 00:35:00.932
They advance in mighty processions:

00:35:00.932 --> 00:35:02.366
beggars and the rich,

00:35:02.366 --> 00:35:03.801
commoners and kings,

00:35:03.801 --> 00:35:05.870
the Church and the popes.

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:10.508
All share the same fear, cries, and trembling,

00:35:10.541 --> 00:35:14.579
for before God, no one is innocent.”

00:35:26.224 --> 00:35:28.659
Yet within this thunderous march,

00:35:28.659 --> 00:35:31.229
we can almost hear, beneath humanity’s fear,

00:35:31.229 --> 00:35:33.931
a fury too intense to be concealed.

00:35:33.931 --> 00:35:36.234
Just like the cry of rage from the first movement:

00:35:36.234 --> 00:35:39.770
Have we not already suffered enough in this world?

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:42.940
Why must we endure such punishment even after death?

00:35:42.940 --> 00:35:44.775
And must the meaning of our lifelong struggle

00:35:44.775 --> 00:35:47.712
also be decided for us by this judgment?

00:35:47.712 --> 00:35:49.881
How unbearably tragic is that?

00:35:49.881 --> 00:35:52.984
And so, when this thunderous march reaches its climax,

00:35:52.984 --> 00:35:55.119
just as in the first movement,

00:35:55.119 --> 00:35:57.255
it collapses once again.

00:36:37.161 --> 00:36:39.397
After the collapse finally settles down,

00:36:39.397 --> 00:36:44.001
we hear the Crying Motif return once more.

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:51.676
This passage of weeping is written brilliantly.

00:36:51.676 --> 00:36:53.311
It is even fiercer than before,

00:36:53.311 --> 00:36:55.213
and even more intense.

00:36:55.213 --> 00:36:56.681
As Mahler himself explained,

00:36:56.681 --> 00:37:01.052
the cries for mercy and forgiveness sound terrifying.

00:37:01.052 --> 00:37:03.421
The wailing grows ever more dreadful.

00:37:03.421 --> 00:37:05.456
As the eternal judgment draws near,

00:37:05.456 --> 00:37:07.358
our senses abandon us,

00:37:07.358 --> 00:37:10.161
and all consciousness fades away.

00:37:17.001 --> 00:37:19.470
At the same time, behind the weeping,

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:22.340
you can still hear endless trumpet calls in the distance.

00:37:22.340 --> 00:37:24.208
It brings to mind two completely different passages

00:37:24.208 --> 00:37:26.811
interwoven within the “Dies Irae.”

00:37:26.811 --> 00:37:29.146
“The trumpet scatters its dreadful sound

00:37:29.146 --> 00:37:30.748
through every tomb on earth,

00:37:30.748 --> 00:37:33.317
summoning all before the throne.”

00:37:33.317 --> 00:37:35.119
And then another passage in complete contrast:

00:37:35.119 --> 00:37:37.221
“What shall I, wretched, say then?

00:37:37.221 --> 00:37:39.390
If even the righteous are scarcely safe,

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:41.892
to whom shall I turn for help?”

00:37:41.892 --> 00:37:46.597
And so the soaring trumpet calls intertwine with the agonized cries of weeping,

00:37:46.597 --> 00:37:50.534
creating something profoundly overwhelming through Mahler’s astonishing writing.

00:38:31.208 --> 00:38:34.078
When sorrow and terror finally reach their absolute peak,

00:38:34.078 --> 00:38:36.447
we are fully prepared to face judgment.

00:38:36.447 --> 00:38:38.849
But then, unexpectedly,

00:38:41.886 --> 00:38:45.790
the raging music suddenly falls silent.

00:38:56.434 --> 00:38:58.402
Mahler himself explained it this way:

00:38:58.402 --> 00:39:01.072
“The final trumpet fades into the distance.

00:39:01.072 --> 00:39:05.176
Its echoes continue to resound through the air.

00:39:05.176 --> 00:39:07.978
And within the strange silence that follows,

00:39:07.978 --> 00:39:12.183
we hear nothing but the song of a nightingale far away.

00:39:12.183 --> 00:39:15.820
It is the final echo of earthly life.”

00:39:15.820 --> 00:39:17.288
And so we hear

00:39:17.288 --> 00:39:20.524
notes played by the flute and piccolo

00:39:20.524 --> 00:39:24.128
transform into the song of a nightingale in the sky.

00:39:41.278 --> 00:39:43.948
The distant trumpets continue sounding,

00:39:43.948 --> 00:39:45.950
but what is happening? Where is the judgment?

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:47.251
Why has it not arrived?

00:39:47.251 --> 00:39:49.053
All we hear is the song of the nightingale,

00:39:49.053 --> 00:39:51.422
so free and weightless

00:39:51.422 --> 00:39:54.425
that it makes us forget all fear.

00:40:01.031 --> 00:40:04.135
At this moment, we arrive at the most crucial turning point of the entire work.

00:40:04.135 --> 00:40:08.606
Mahler is finally about to reveal his answer to death itself.

00:40:08.606 --> 00:40:09.974
According to Mahler,

00:40:09.974 --> 00:40:12.143
he searched for this answer for a very long time.

00:40:12.143 --> 00:40:14.845
Until he attended the funeral of Hans von Bülow,

00:40:14.845 --> 00:40:18.149
one of Europe’s most prominent musicians and his esteemed senior mentor.

00:40:18.149 --> 00:40:23.387
It was there, upon hearing the poet Klopstock’s hymn "Resurrection," that it suddenly dawned on him.

00:40:23.387 --> 00:40:26.357
So, *this* is what death is truly about.

00:40:26.357 --> 00:40:27.992
And so, in the final recapitulation,

00:40:27.992 --> 00:40:31.929
the long-silent chorus finally begins to sing.

00:40:31.929 --> 00:40:35.433
Through the human voice, Mahler directly conveys to us

00:40:35.433 --> 00:40:38.235
the ultimate answer he had finally discovered.

00:42:28.012 --> 00:42:30.080
Indeed, if we are to rise again,

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:32.583
then why should we fear death?

00:42:32.583 --> 00:42:35.653
Why should we fear losing the meaning of life?

00:42:35.653 --> 00:42:39.390
Hearing this poem, Mahler felt as though he had been struck by a bolt of lightning.

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:41.825
Thus, he decided to adopt Klopstock’s poem,

00:42:41.825 --> 00:42:43.727
pairing it with this Resurrection Motif

00:42:43.727 --> 00:42:47.197
to serve as the final answer to this long-standing inquiry.

00:42:47.197 --> 00:42:49.266
Yet, the concept of resurrection has existed

00:42:49.266 --> 00:42:51.068
within Christian dogma for thousands of years;

00:42:51.068 --> 00:42:52.836
it was by no means a new discovery.

00:42:52.836 --> 00:42:55.406
Did Mahler achieve some brand-new realization here?

00:42:55.406 --> 00:42:57.708
And could it be that after struggling in agony for so long,

00:42:57.708 --> 00:43:01.245
the final conclusion is simply a return to conventional faith?

00:43:01.245 --> 00:43:02.846
Of course, it isn’t that simple.

00:43:02.846 --> 00:43:04.548
You can observe several key details.

00:43:04.548 --> 00:43:07.351
Although Mahler quoted Klopstock’s religious hymn,

00:43:07.351 --> 00:43:10.187
he only used the first two stanzas;

00:43:10.187 --> 00:43:12.656
the rest of the content was completely rewritten by himself.

00:43:12.656 --> 00:43:14.291
Why is that?

00:43:14.291 --> 00:43:15.492
And there is another detail.

00:43:15.492 --> 00:43:16.226
Do you still remember?

00:43:16.226 --> 00:43:17.461
Earlier, we kept emphasizing

00:43:17.461 --> 00:43:19.296
that in traditional Christian doctrine,

00:43:19.296 --> 00:43:20.998
resurrection is for judgment—

00:43:20.998 --> 00:43:23.233
there must be judgment before you can ascend to heaven.

00:43:23.233 --> 00:43:26.003
Hence, when we listened to the orchestra’s exposition earlier,

00:43:26.003 --> 00:43:29.306
the Resurrection Motif was invariably accompanied by the "Dies Irae."

00:43:29.306 --> 00:43:31.775
Yet here, in the chorus’s recapitulation,

00:43:31.775 --> 00:43:33.777
we hear only the Resurrection Motif,

00:43:33.777 --> 00:43:36.814
while the "Dies Irae" has miraculously vanished.

00:43:36.814 --> 00:43:38.082
Why is that?

00:43:38.082 --> 00:43:39.617
I believe it is because

00:43:39.617 --> 00:43:41.385
the resurrection Mahler envisioned

00:43:41.385 --> 00:43:43.520
merely borrowed the shell of Christian religion,

00:43:43.520 --> 00:43:46.490
while injecting a completely new spirit inside.

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:48.759
Though Mahler was a Catholic convert,

00:43:48.759 --> 00:43:51.862
his relationship with faith was actually highly complex.

00:43:51.862 --> 00:43:53.364
His thoughts regarding life

00:43:53.364 --> 00:43:56.100
were fused with many philosophical currents of the 19th century.

00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:57.635
The Church of old told us

00:43:57.635 --> 00:43:59.370
that humanity was created by God,

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:01.505
and we exist solely because of Him,

00:44:01.505 --> 00:44:04.441
so the meaning of life is to obey His will—

00:44:04.441 --> 00:44:07.311
constantly repenting, glorifying God, and facing judgment.

00:44:07.311 --> 00:44:09.647
But by the 19th century in which Mahler grew up,

00:44:09.647 --> 00:44:13.217
many thinkers—like Kant, Goethe, Schopenhauer, and Nietzsche—

00:44:13.217 --> 00:44:16.920
had begun to reconsider the relationship between man and faith.

00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:19.890
Take the character of "Faust" in Goethe’s masterpiece:

00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:21.225
tempted by the devil,

00:44:21.225 --> 00:44:23.527
he traded his soul and made a deal with him,

00:44:23.527 --> 00:44:25.396
and even committed numerous transgressions.

00:44:25.396 --> 00:44:27.331
In traditional Christian morality,

00:44:27.331 --> 00:44:29.967
he would surely be struck down by lightning and cast into hell.

00:44:29.967 --> 00:44:31.568
Yet under Goethe’s pen,

00:44:31.568 --> 00:44:35.839
Faust is ultimately saved and enters heaven, because despite being tempted,

00:44:35.839 --> 00:44:37.975
he continuously strived to better the world

00:44:37.975 --> 00:44:39.710
and pursue higher ideals.

00:44:39.710 --> 00:44:43.547
Hence, you hear Goethe state through the angels:

00:44:43.547 --> 00:44:45.649
"Whoever strives upward with all his might,

00:44:45.649 --> 00:44:48.085
that all of us are capable of salvation.

00:44:48.085 --> 00:44:49.787
And Nietzsche went even further.

00:44:49.787 --> 00:44:52.189
He directly told us to forget God,

00:44:52.189 --> 00:44:55.292
because we should not place the meaning of life in some world beyond,

00:44:55.292 --> 00:44:57.227
or in those vague and distant gods.

00:44:57.227 --> 00:45:00.164
The thing that gives meaning to your life should not be someone else.

00:45:00.164 --> 00:45:01.765
It should be yourself.

00:45:01.765 --> 00:45:03.834
You must fight for it yourself,

00:45:03.834 --> 00:45:04.802
struggle for it yourself.

00:45:04.802 --> 00:45:08.806
In the face of life’s endless suffering, ugliness, cruelty, and darkness,

00:45:08.806 --> 00:45:11.508
rather than resenting a creator who may not even exist,

00:45:11.508 --> 00:45:14.144
you should bravely confront suffering,

00:45:14.144 --> 00:45:15.546
love your fate,

00:45:15.546 --> 00:45:17.581
and love everything in life.

00:45:17.581 --> 00:45:19.249
Because only by experiencing such suffering

00:45:19.249 --> 00:45:21.285
can you transcend yourself

00:45:21.285 --> 00:45:23.520
and create new values.

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:24.421
Just like Beethoven:

00:45:24.421 --> 00:45:26.690
faced with the despair of losing his hearing,

00:45:26.690 --> 00:45:28.358
he still refused to give up on life,

00:45:28.358 --> 00:45:32.463
and was ultimately able to compose music as great as the Ninth Symphony.

00:45:32.463 --> 00:45:38.235
When we hear this music, it gives us a sense of joy and wonder that transcends everything in the world.

00:45:38.235 --> 00:45:40.671
It makes us feel that even if Schopenhauer may have been right,

00:45:40.671 --> 00:45:43.073
that life is indeed suffering at its core,

00:45:43.073 --> 00:45:45.642
Instead, we should raise our glasses and cry out:

00:45:45.642 --> 00:45:49.413
“O life! Once more!”

00:45:49.413 --> 00:45:51.448
Clearly, Mahler was influenced by these philosophers from a young age,

00:45:51.448 --> 00:45:55.052
forming his own view of life

00:45:55.052 --> 00:45:58.355
and pouring the essence of these ideas into his works.

00:45:58.355 --> 00:46:00.190
For example, in his Third Symphony,

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:01.925
he directly quoted Nietzsche.

00:46:01.925 --> 00:46:05.062
And in his Eighth Symphony is based on Goethe’s Faust.

00:46:05.062 --> 00:46:07.164
And this Second Symphony is no exception.

00:46:07.164 --> 00:46:09.299
It’s just that, unlike Nietzsche,

00:46:09.299 --> 00:46:11.101
he did not choose to radically deny the existence of God.

00:46:11.101 --> 00:46:13.103
Because a world without faith

00:46:13.103 --> 00:46:15.339
is simply too empty, too despairing.

00:46:15.339 --> 00:46:17.674
Not everyone can bear it.

00:46:17.674 --> 00:46:21.879
For Mahler, perhaps he would still rather believe

00:46:21.879 --> 00:46:22.813
that in the world beyond,

00:46:22.813 --> 00:46:25.849
there is a God waiting for us with infinite love.

00:46:25.849 --> 00:46:28.552
But at the same time, like Nietzsche,

00:46:28.552 --> 00:46:31.121
Mahler places the authority to determine life’s meaning

00:46:31.121 --> 00:46:32.990
back into our own hands.

00:46:32.990 --> 00:46:34.424
As he himself explained:

00:46:34.424 --> 00:46:36.360
“The final trumpet call has ended,

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:39.296
yet none of what was expected occurs.

00:46:39.296 --> 00:46:41.632
There is no judgment, no sinners,

00:46:41.632 --> 00:46:44.802
no righteous, no great men, no petty men,

00:46:44.802 --> 00:46:46.937
no punishment, and no reward.”

00:46:46.937 --> 00:46:49.406
An almighty feeling of love fills us,

00:46:49.406 --> 00:46:54.444
bringing us a blissful realization and illuminating our existence.

00:46:54.444 --> 00:46:55.846
Do you see the core point?

00:46:55.846 --> 00:46:58.682
This is exactly why those terrifying "Dies Irae" elements

00:46:58.682 --> 00:47:00.951
completely vanish at this point.

00:47:00.951 --> 00:47:03.587
Because we no longer need that judgment to dictate

00:47:03.587 --> 00:47:05.055
whether we may enter heaven;

00:47:05.055 --> 00:47:07.558
rather, we must strive and conquer on our own,

00:47:07.558 --> 00:47:10.327
winning our own wings to take flight into the sky,

00:47:10.327 --> 00:47:13.864
pursuing that light which no eye has ever seen.

00:47:13.864 --> 00:47:16.099
Understanding these prerequisites, you will know

00:47:16.099 --> 00:47:19.903
why Mahler had to rewrite Klopstock's religious verses.

00:47:19.903 --> 00:47:24.074
When Mahler first heard this poem, although the opening lines shook him profoundly,

00:47:24.074 --> 00:47:27.678
the subsequent text was far too explicitly religious and conservative.

00:47:27.678 --> 00:47:31.415
So Mahler simply discarded them all and wrote his own version.

00:47:31.415 --> 00:47:34.651
In these rewritten passages, not only is the religious tone heavily diminished,

00:47:34.651 --> 00:47:38.989
but it can also be said to encapsulate the very essence of Mahler's agonizing 
contemplation.

00:47:38.989 --> 00:47:41.558
For instance, in this initial section, he says:

00:47:41.558 --> 00:47:43.694
"Oh believe, my heart,

00:47:43.694 --> 00:47:46.029
nothing is lost to you!

00:47:46.029 --> 00:47:49.566
Yours is what you longed for,

00:47:49.566 --> 00:47:53.237
yours, what you loved,

00:47:53.237 --> 00:47:57.474
what you fought for!

00:47:57.474 --> 00:48:00.811
Oh believe: you were not born in vain!

00:48:00.811 --> 00:48:04.348
Have not lived and suffered in vain!"

00:48:04.348 --> 00:48:06.617
What a colossal power these words exude!

00:48:06.617 --> 00:48:11.488
It even allows you to feel an intense love and affirmation of life akin to Nietzsche's philosophy.

00:48:11.488 --> 00:48:14.958
And as it nears the end, these emotions grow increasingly powerful and spectacular.

00:48:14.958 --> 00:48:16.460
For example, in the following section,

00:48:16.460 --> 00:48:19.796
you will hear the soprano and alto voices clashing fiercely with one another.

00:48:19.796 --> 00:48:20.664
One says:

00:48:20.664 --> 00:48:26.403
"Oh Pain, you piercer of all things! From you I have been wrested!"

00:48:26.403 --> 00:48:27.704
While the other declares:

00:48:27.704 --> 00:48:34.244
"Oh Death, you conqueror of all things! Now you are conquered!"

00:48:34.244 --> 00:48:37.648
These two powerful lines transform into a canon between the two parts,

00:48:37.648 --> 00:48:40.817
intertwining violently and continuously ascending,

00:48:40.817 --> 00:48:42.552
utterly staggering.

00:49:30.033 --> 00:49:32.769
Then we hear the repeating, iterative Soaring Motif,

00:49:32.769 --> 00:49:34.571
just as the lyrics proclaim:

00:49:34.571 --> 00:49:35.772
"With wings

00:49:35.772 --> 00:49:38.175
which I have won for myself,

00:49:38.175 --> 00:49:39.943
I shall soar upwards!"

00:52:08.758 --> 00:52:08.792
Just as Mahler himself noted:

00:52:08.792 --> 00:52:10.627
Just as Mahler himself noted:

00:52:10.627 --> 00:52:14.064
"A symphony must be like the world.

00:52:14.064 --> 00:52:16.633
It must embrace everything."

00:52:16.633 --> 00:52:19.369
This Symphony No. 2 is certainly no exception.

00:52:19.369 --> 00:52:23.473
What it embraces is not merely the joy and happiness in life,

00:52:23.473 --> 00:52:27.144
but also all the suffering and sorrow.

00:52:27.144 --> 00:52:29.479
Even if the destination of life is nothingness,

00:52:29.479 --> 00:52:30.814
so what?

00:52:30.814 --> 00:52:33.150
The most beautiful scenery of any journey

00:52:33.150 --> 00:52:35.018
is never its final destination,

00:52:35.018 --> 00:52:37.220
but the sweat and tears you shed

00:52:37.220 --> 00:52:40.624
during the struggle along the way.

00:52:40.624 --> 00:52:43.193
Only when you have fought with your own hands

00:52:43.193 --> 00:52:44.327
and suffered,

00:52:44.327 --> 00:52:47.931
will you truly feel that you have once lived.

00:52:51.801 --> 00:52:51.835
Yeah!

00:52:51.835 --> 00:52:53.737
Yeah!

00:52:53.737 --> 00:52:55.405
Thank you everyone for watching this video.

00:52:55.405 --> 00:52:56.940
This video has broken a record,

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:58.308
becoming the single longest episode

00:52:58.308 --> 00:53:00.443
in our channel's history.

00:53:00.443 --> 00:53:03.046
Lastly, I want to specifically mention one thing:

00:53:03.046 --> 00:53:09.052
Have you noticed the animation production for this episode, right?

00:53:09.052 --> 00:53:11.054
Isn't it incredibly refined and splendid?

00:53:11.054 --> 00:53:15.292
For this, I want to extend a massive thank you to our three motion graphic designers:

00:53:15.292 --> 00:53:17.260
Fred, Wendy, and Vicky.

00:53:17.260 --> 00:53:18.695
They are truly remarkable.

00:53:18.695 --> 00:53:20.497
For such a long video,

00:53:20.497 --> 00:53:23.200
and without any actual live footage of an orchestra performing,

00:53:23.200 --> 00:53:25.669
they managed to make the visuals so captivating!

00:53:25.669 --> 00:53:28.004
It is clear that they poured an immense amount of effort into it,

00:53:28.004 --> 00:53:31.641
and also put up with a lot of my stubborn demands.

00:53:31.641 --> 00:53:32.909
So I am incredibly grateful to them.

00:53:32.909 --> 00:53:34.044
If you guys like it,

00:53:34.044 --> 00:53:36.780
please leave them lots of encouraging words in the comment section below!

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:38.515
Additionally, I want to express my gratitude once again to Weiwuying

00:53:38.515 --> 00:53:42.652
for giving us such a wonderful opportunity to produce a video that I feel is deeply meaningful.

00:53:42.652 --> 00:53:44.287
Now, if you were moved by this,

00:53:44.287 --> 00:53:45.589
remember to go buy tickets!

00:53:45.589 --> 00:53:49.626
Sir Simon Rattle leading the Symphonieorchester des Bayerischen Rundfunks in Kaohsiung—

00:53:49.626 --> 00:53:51.494
the first performance sold out immediately upon release,

00:53:51.494 --> 00:53:53.196
so they added a second one.

00:53:53.196 --> 00:53:55.398
I wonder if this one will also sell out just as quickly.

00:53:55.398 --> 00:53:56.700
Anyway, hurry up and go grab your tickets!

00:53:56.700 --> 00:53:58.335
In the future, you can also keep a close eye on Weiwuying;

00:53:58.335 --> 00:54:00.670
they have fantastic performances hitting the stage every single year.

00:54:00.670 --> 00:54:02.539
So, in conclusion, thank you for watching this video.

00:54:02.539 --> 00:54:04.841
If you like our content, please share it around!

00:54:04.841 --> 00:54:09.012
You can also go support our classical music online course: "Revolution on the Score."

00:54:09.012 --> 00:54:10.513
